              <div class="post-faq">
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Do I legally need a licensed contractor for a small home repair in Pennsylvania?</h3>
                  <p>Not necessarily. If the work is a small, self-contained repair and the total amount you pay that person or business in a calendar year stays under $5,000, HICPA registration isn't legally required of them. Once that threshold is crossed, or once the work requires a building permit, you're in general contractor territory.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>How do I check if a contractor or handyman is actually registered in Pennsylvania?</h3>
                  <p>Use the PA Attorney General's Home Improvement Contractor registry and search by business name or HIC number. This free public tool shows current registration status.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Can a handyman legally do electrical or plumbing work in Delaware County?</h3>
                  <p>Generally, no, not beyond very minor fixes that don't require a permit, such as replacing a fixture on existing wiring. New circuits, panel work, gas lines, and most plumbing beyond simple fixture swaps typically require a licensed electrician or plumber.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>What happens if I hire an unlicensed person for work that required a permit?</h3>
                  <p>You may have difficulty getting the work inspected and approved after the fact, you could be required to open up completed work for inspection, and unpermitted work can create problems at resale.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Is it cheaper to hire a handyman and just skip the permit for a small project?</h3>
                  <p>It might look cheaper upfront, but skipping a required permit carries real risk: the work may not meet code, it can create liability if something fails, and it becomes a disclosure issue when you eventually sell the home.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Who do I contact if I have a complaint about a home improvement contractor in PA?</h3>
                  <p>Complaints related to HICPA violations can be filed with the PA Attorney General's Bureau of Consumer Protection. The Better Business Bureau is also a useful resource.</p>
                </div>
